<Docs slug="catalogs">
  <p>
    A catalog is a named list of folders: the folders in the Folders panel on
    the left. Keep one for family photos and another for client work, and
    switch between them. LightPhotos opens the catalog you used last, or one
    called <strong>default</strong> the first time.
  </p>

  <p>
    The open catalog's name sits in the header, after the LightPhotos
    wordmark. Click it for the catalog menu.
  </p>

  <ul class="lp-article-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Make a catalog.</strong> Choose <strong>New Catalog&hellip;</strong>
      from the catalog menu and give it a name. The desktop app asks where to
      save it, starting in <code>~/Pictures/LightPhotos/</code>. A new
      catalog has no folders; click
      <strong>Add Folder</strong> above the Folders list to fill it. The
      desktop app also has <strong>New Catalog&hellip;</strong> in the macOS
      File menu.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Switch catalogs.</strong> The catalog menu lists your recent
      catalogs, newest first. Click one to open it, and the Folders panel
      shows that catalog's folders.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Open a catalog from disk.</strong> In the desktop app, choose
      <strong>Open Catalog&hellip;</strong> and pick a
      <code>.lightphotos</code> folder. Use this for a catalog you copied
      from another computer or keep somewhere other than the usual place.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Add and remove folders.</strong> Adding or removing a folder
      changes only the open catalog. The same folder can be in several
      catalogs. Removing it from one never touches its photos or edits.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What a catalog holds</h2>
  <p>
    Three things: the list of folders, the list of
    <a href="/docs/presets.html">preset folders</a>, and a copy of the star
    ratings, flags and color labels of the photos in them. Develop edits and
    thumbnails are not in the catalog. They stay in the
    <code>.lightphotos</code> folder next to your photos, as before. Your
    own presets, under My Presets, are shared by every catalog.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your folders are still the real record. LightPhotos writes every rating
    and edit next to the photo first, and the catalog's copy follows. If you
    delete a catalog, you lose its list of folders, not your work. Add the
    folders to another catalog and everything is there.
  </p>
  <h2>What it is for: search</h2>
  <p>
    LightPhotos will use what the catalog stores to search and filter across
    every folder in it at once, for example every five-star photo or every
    pick, without opening each folder. Today the Grid still shows one folder
    at a time and nothing reads the copy yet; search comes in a later
    release.
  </p>

  <h2>Where catalogs are stored</h2>
  <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
    <table class="lp-compare">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">App</th>
          <th scope="col">Where it goes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Desktop</strong></td>
          <td>A folder named <code>&lt;name&gt;.lightphotos</code>, by default in <code>~/Pictures/LightPhotos/</code></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Web</strong></td>
          <td>Your browser's storage for this site. Each browser has its own catalogs.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    The web app has no <strong>Open Catalog&hellip;</strong>, because a
    browser cannot keep a catalog in a folder on disk. Its catalog menu lists
    every catalog made in that browser. If you clear the site's data, the
    browser forgets its catalogs and its access to your folders. Your photos
    and edits stay in the folders.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the last catalog cannot be read at launch, LightPhotos opens
    <strong>default</strong> instead and says so. It tries the last one again at the next launch.
  </p>
</Docs>
